{
  "requirement": "Repository validation MUST continue after a failed check and report every failed\ncheck it finds in one run, each as a {term}`requirements.ci.validation_error`.\nThis is a convention for every validation error; the requirement for each check\nstates which conditions that check reports. A file that cannot be parsed, such as\na malformed `habeas.toml`, `book.toml`, or evidence file, is one failed check:\nits error MAY report only the first parse or decoding problem in that file, and\nchecks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict.** The behaviour is largely implemented and tested. The `{impl}` marking is incomplete, so I judge this a gap rather than a pass.\n\n**What is implemented and marked `{impl}`**\n- `inspect_repository_with_progress` (src/evidence.rs:178-330) records every failure in one `errors` vector and never returns early on a validation problem.\n  - An invalid `habeas.toml` is recorded as one error, and the run continues.\n  - Missing, orphaned and malformed evidence files are each reported, and the later phases still run.\n- `book_source_dir` (src/evidence.rs:332-386) reports a malformed `book.toml` as one error and falls back to `md`, so later checks still run.\n- `collect_definitions` and `files_with_extension` push an error and `continue`, so one bad definition or directory does not hide the others.\n- `validate_evidence_file` (src/evidence.rs:619-755) reports only the first parse problem for a malformed evidence file and returns. A well-formed record reports every failed field check.\n- `close_dependencies` and `validate_dependencies` (src/terms.rs) report one problem per unresolvable term.\n- `main` lets `status` and `info` run despite an invalid `habeas.toml`, so the report is still produced.\n\n**Tests**\n- `configuration_and_dependency_failures_do_not_stop_later_checks` covers an invalid `habeas.toml`, an invalid `book.toml`, a malformed evidence file, and all unresolved terms. It also checks that other records' errors and source-reference errors still appear.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` covers many simultaneous failures in one run.\n- `inspection_progress_preserves_validation_and_finishes_each_phase` covers broken JSON, an unparsable Rust file, and an uncited function.\n- `status_loads_nearest_workspace_file_without_validating_authoring` (tests/commands.rs) covers the CLI path.\n- The tests are good. They do not cover a malformed citation-review file or an unreadable chapter.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Unmarked fallback in `DiscoverySources::load`** (src/authoring/access.rs, `PathPolicy::load(root).unwrap_or_default()`).\n   - This is the code that lets reference discovery keep running when `habeas.toml` is invalid. The doc comment says so: \"reported by inspection itself ... rather than failing a second time\".\n   - It is exactly the \"checks that do not depend on its contents MUST still run\" obligation, and the test above exercises it.\n   - It carries only `references.targets` and `authoring.deterministic_validation`, with no `{impl}` for `requirements.ci.errors`.\n2. **`citations::validate_review` is not marked for `ci.errors`.** It treats a malformed citation-review file, which is an evidence file, as one failed check: it pushes an error and returns for unreadable files, invalid JSON, a non-object, or a missing `inputs` array. It is marked only for `references.citation_review`, `ci.outcomes` and `ci.diagnostics`.\n3. **Other continue-and-collect code is not marked.** `validate_references`, `collect_references_with_progress`, and `rust_coverage::validate_rust_functions` all carry out the \"continue and report every failure\" convention. None is marked `{impl}` for `ci.errors`. Neither is the malformed-Rust-file handling, which reports \"cannot parse Rust source\" and continues.\n\nBecause the requirement is a convention for every validation error, code that carries it out should be marked. Items 1 and 2 are clear cases of such code without a marker. No FIXME acknowledges these gaps, so the result is false rather than FIXME.",
